# The Complete Guide to Game Audio, Polish & Publishing

Audio and polish transform a functional game into a memorable experience. Combined with a solid publishing strategy, these elements determine whether your indie game reaches its audience.

## Game Audio Fundamentals

### Audio System Architecture

```
Audio Categories:
  Music:
    - Background tracks
    - Adaptive/dynamic music
    - Stingers (short musical hits)

  Sound Effects (SFX):
    - Player actions (jump, attack, collect)
    - Environment (wind, rain, ambient)
    - UI feedback (button clicks, notifications)

  Voice:
    - Dialogue
    - Narration
    - Character barks (short exclamations)
```

### Audio Implementation in Unity

```csharp
// Audio Manager singleton
public class AudioManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public static AudioManager Instance { get; private set; }

    [Header("Audio Sources")]
    [SerializeField] private AudioSource musicSource;
    [SerializeField] private AudioSource sfxSource;

    [Header("Audio Clips")]
    [SerializeField] private AudioClip[] musicTracks;
    [SerializeField] private AudioClip[] jumpSounds;
    [SerializeField] private AudioClip[] hitSounds;

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (Instance == null)
        {
            Instance = this;
            DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
        }
        else Destroy(gameObject);
    }

    public void PlaySFX(AudioClip[] clips, float volume = 1f)
    {
        if (clips.Length == 0) return;
        // Random variation prevents repetitive sounds
        AudioClip clip = clips[Random.Range(0, clips.Length)];
        sfxSource.pitch = Random.Range(0.9f, 1.1f);
        sfxSource.PlayOneShot(clip, volume);
    }

    public void PlayMusic(int trackIndex)
    {
        StartCoroutine(CrossfadeMusic(musicTracks[trackIndex]));
    }

    private IEnumerator CrossfadeMusic(AudioClip newTrack)
    {
        float duration = 1.5f;
        float timer = 0;
        float startVolume = musicSource.volume;

        // Fade out
        while (timer < duration)
        {
            timer += Time.deltaTime;
            musicSource.volume = Mathf.Lerp(startVolume, 0, timer / duration);
            yield return null;
        }

        // Switch track
        musicSource.clip = newTrack;
        musicSource.Play();

        // Fade in
        timer = 0;
        while (timer < duration)
        {
            timer += Time.deltaTime;
            musicSource.volume = Mathf.Lerp(0, startVolume, timer / duration);
            yield return null;
        }
    }
}

// Usage
AudioManager.Instance.PlaySFX(jumpSounds, 0.8f);
```

### Dynamic Music System

```csharp
// Adaptive music that responds to gameplay
public class DynamicMusic : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private AudioSource explorationLayer;
    [SerializeField] private AudioSource combatLayer;
    [SerializeField] private AudioSource tensionLayer;

    private float combatIntensity = 0f;

    void Start()
    {
        // All layers play simultaneously, synced
        explorationLayer.Play();
        combatLayer.Play();
        tensionLayer.Play();

        combatLayer.volume = 0;
        tensionLayer.volume = 0;
    }

    public void SetCombatIntensity(float intensity)
    {
        combatIntensity = Mathf.Clamp01(intensity);
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // Smoothly blend between layers
        float target = combatIntensity;
        explorationLayer.volume = Mathf.Lerp(
            explorationLayer.volume,
            1f - target,
            Time.deltaTime * 2f
        );
        combatLayer.volume = Mathf.Lerp(
            combatLayer.volume,
            target,
            Time.deltaTime * 2f
        );
    }
}
```

### Audio Best Practices

```
1. Variation: Use 3-5 variants per sound effect
2. Pitch Randomization: ±5-10% for natural feel
3. Spatial Audio: Use 3D sounds for positional awareness
4. Mixing: Music -12dB, SFX -6dB, UI at reference
5. Compression: Use .ogg for long audio, .wav for short SFX
6. Ducking: Lower music volume during dialogue
7. Silence: Strategic silence can be more powerful than sound
```

## Game Polish & "Juice"

Polish is the collection of small details that make a game feel great.

### Screen Shake

```csharp
public class CameraShake : MonoBehaviour
{
    private Vector3 originalPos;
    private float shakeTimer = 0;
    private float shakeMagnitude = 0;

    void Start()
    {
        originalPos = transform.localPosition;
    }

    public void Shake(float duration, float magnitude)
    {
        shakeTimer = duration;
        shakeMagnitude = magnitude;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (shakeTimer > 0)
        {
            float x = Random.Range(-1f, 1f) * shakeMagnitude;
            float y = Random.Range(-1f, 1f) * shakeMagnitude;
            transform.localPosition = originalPos + new Vector3(x, y, 0);

            shakeTimer -= Time.deltaTime;
            // Decay magnitude over time
            shakeMagnitude *= 0.9f;
        }
        else
        {
            transform.localPosition = originalPos;
        }
    }
}

// Usage: On enemy hit
CameraShake.Instance.Shake(0.2f, 0.3f);
```

### Hit Pause (Freeze Frame)

```csharp
public class HitPause : MonoBehaviour
{
    public static HitPause Instance;

    public void Pause(float duration)
    {
        StartCoroutine(DoPause(duration));
    }

    private IEnumerator DoPause(float duration)
    {
        Time.timeScale = 0.05f; // Near-freeze
        yield return new WaitForSecondsRealtime(duration);
        Time.timeScale = 1f;
    }
}

// On powerful hit: 50ms freeze frame
HitPause.Instance.Pause(0.05f);
```

### Particle Effects

```csharp
// Spawn particles on events
public class EffectsManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private ParticleSystem hitParticles;
    [SerializeField] private ParticleSystem dustParticles;
    [SerializeField] private ParticleSystem collectParticles;

    public void SpawnHitEffect(Vector3 position, Vector3 normal)
    {
        var particles = Instantiate(hitParticles, position,
            Quaternion.LookRotation(normal));
        particles.Play();
        Destroy(particles.gameObject, 2f);
    }

    public void SpawnDustPuff(Vector3 position)
    {
        var particles = Instantiate(dustParticles, position,
            Quaternion.identity);
        particles.Play();
        Destroy(particles.gameObject, 1f);
    }
}
```

### Squash and Stretch

```csharp
// Apply squash/stretch to sprites for lively animation
public class SquashStretch : MonoBehaviour
{
    private Vector3 originalScale;
    private float animTimer = 0;

    void Start()
    {
        originalScale = transform.localScale;
    }

    public void DoSquash(float amount = 0.3f, float duration = 0.15f)
    {
        StartCoroutine(Animate(
            new Vector3(1 + amount, 1 - amount, 1),
            duration
        ));
    }

    public void DoStretch(float amount = 0.3f, float duration = 0.15f)
    {
        StartCoroutine(Animate(
            new Vector3(1 - amount, 1 + amount, 1),
            duration
        ));
    }

    private IEnumerator Animate(Vector3 targetScale, float duration)
    {
        float timer = 0;
        Vector3 start = originalScale;
        Vector3 target = Vector3.Scale(originalScale, targetScale);

        while (timer < duration)
        {
            timer += Time.deltaTime;
            float t = timer / duration;
            // Elastic ease out
            t = 1 - Mathf.Pow(1 - t, 3);
            transform.localScale = Vector3.Lerp(start, target, t);
            yield return null;
        }

        // Return to original
        timer = 0;
        while (timer < duration)
        {
            timer += Time.deltaTime;
            float t = timer / duration;
            transform.localScale = Vector3.Lerp(target, originalScale, t);
            yield return null;
        }

        transform.localScale = originalScale;
    }
}

// On landing: squash. On jumping: stretch.
```

### Polish Checklist

```
Visual Polish:
  ✅ Screen shake on impacts
  ✅ Hit pause on powerful attacks
  ✅ Particle effects for actions
  ✅ Squash and stretch on characters
  ✅ Trail effects on fast movement
  ✅ Flash white on damage
  ✅ Smooth camera follow with lerp
  ✅ UI animations (bounce, fade, slide)

Audio Polish:
  ✅ Sound for every player action
  ✅ Variation in sound effects
  ✅ Music that matches the mood
  ✅ Ambient environmental sounds
  ✅ UI click/hover sounds
  ✅ Victory/defeat jingles

Gameplay Polish:
  ✅ Coyote time (jump grace period)
  ✅ Input buffering
  ✅ Generous hitboxes for player
  ✅ Clear visual feedback for all actions
  ✅ Responsive controls (< 100ms input lag)
```

## Publishing Your Game

### Pre-Launch Preparation

```
Marketing Assets:
  - Store page screenshots (5-10)
  - Gameplay trailer (60-90 seconds)
  - Capsule art (various sizes)
  - Press kit (logo, screenshots, description)
  - GIFs for social media

Timeline:
  6 months before: Start building wishlists
  3 months before: Release trailer
  1 month before: Send press keys
  2 weeks before: Final build testing
  Launch day: Community engagement
  1 week after: First patch based on feedback
```

### Publishing on Steam

```
Steam Requirements:
  1. Steamworks account ($100 fee per app)
  2. Store page setup:
     - Short description (< 300 chars)
     - Long description (HTML supported)
     - Minimum 5 screenshots
     - At least 1 trailer
     - System requirements
     - Content descriptors
  3. Build upload via SteamPipe
  4. Review process (1-5 business days)

Steam Best Practices:
  - Set up Coming Soon page early
  - Gather wishlists (10,000+ for good launch)
  - Use Steam Events for announcements
  - Participate in Steam festivals/sales
  - Release on Tuesday or Thursday
  - Enable achievements and cloud saves
```

### Publishing on itch.io

```
itch.io Advantages:
  - No upfront cost
  - Pay what you want pricing
  - Web builds supported
  - Great for prototypes and game jams
  - Community-friendly

Setup:
  1. Create project page
  2. Upload builds (Windows, Mac, Linux, Web)
  3. Set pricing (free, paid, or PWYW)
  4. Write devlog for updates
  5. Submit to collections and jams
```

### Mobile Publishing

```
iOS App Store:
  - Apple Developer account ($99/year)
  - App Store Review Guidelines compliance
  - Screenshots for each device size
  - App Privacy details
  - Review time: 24-48 hours

Google Play Store:
  - Google Play Console ($25 one-time)
  - Content rating questionnaire
  - Data safety section
  - Review time: hours to days

Mobile Considerations:
  - Touch control optimization
  - Battery consumption testing
  - Various screen sizes/ratios
  - Offline functionality
  - Monetization compliance
```

### Launch Strategy

```
Pre-Launch:
  □ Build email list and Discord community
  □ Share development progress (devlogs, social media)
  □ Send demo/press builds to content creators
  □ Prepare launch day social media posts
  □ Set up analytics tracking

Launch Day:
  □ Announce across all channels simultaneously
  □ Be active in community (Discord, Reddit, Twitter)
  □ Respond to bug reports quickly
  □ Thank early supporters
  □ Monitor reviews and feedback

Post-Launch:
  □ Release Day 1 patch for critical bugs
  □ Engage with community feedback
  □ Plan content updates
  □ Participate in seasonal sales
  □ Track metrics (sales, retention, reviews)
```

## Pricing Strategy

```
Factors to Consider:
  - Development time and costs
  - Content length ($/hour value)
  - Competitor pricing
  - Target audience
  - Platform norms

Common Price Points:
  $0-5:   Short experiences, game jam games
  $5-15:  Standard indie games (2-10 hours)
  $15-25: Premium indie games (10-30 hours)
  $25-40: Large indie/AA games

Tips:
  - Price slightly higher at launch (room for sales)
  - Launch discount of 10-15%
  - Regional pricing matters globally
  - Don't undervalue your work
```

## Conclusion

Polish and audio transform good games into great ones, and a solid publishing strategy ensures they reach their audience.

**Key Takeaways:**
1. Audio adds 50% of the game feel
2. Small polish details compound into a great experience
3. Screen shake, particles, and hitstop are essential juice
4. Start marketing 6 months before launch
5. Choose platforms based on your audience
6. Price fairly and plan for post-launch support

Ship your game!
